# substrata

[![Docs](https://readthedocs.org/projects/substrata/badge/?version=latest)](https://substrata.readthedocs.io/en/latest/)

**substrata** is a Python package for 3-D geometry and point-cloud handling,
built for coral-reef photogrammetry workflows. It turns the outputs of a
structure-from-motion pipeline — dense point clouds, camera poses, and image
annotations — into scaled, georeferenced models that you can measure and
analyze.

## Features

- **Point clouds** — load, decimate, and stream very large PLY files without
  holding them in memory (backed by Open3D).
- **Cameras** — read Metashape camera poses and sensors, match images, extract
  video frames, and time-sync cameras with depth logs.
- **Annotations** — manage labeled features (e.g. individual corals) or generate
  random/stratified point annotations, and move between 2-D image annotations
  and 3-D coordinates to transfer annotations across overlapping reconstructions.
- **Scaling & orientation** — recover real-world scale from scalebars and orient
  a reconstruction into a world coordinate frame.
- **Measurements** — terrain and ecology metrics: TPI/TRI, rugosity, fractal
  dimension, surface area, gap fraction, and depth regression.
- **Visualization** — composite views and QC report PDFs.

A command-line tool (`substrata`) drives the most common workflows directly from
a project directory.

## Installation

substrata depends on several native libraries (Open3D, OpenCV, PyTorch) that are
installed with conda:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/reefscapegenomics/substrata.git
cd substrata
conda env create -f environment.yml
conda activate substrata
python -m pip install -e .
```

See the [installation guide](https://substrata.readthedocs.io/en/latest/installation.html)
for details.

## Documentation

Full documentation — tutorials, command-line usage, and the API reference — is
hosted at [substrata.readthedocs.io](https://substrata.readthedocs.io/en/latest/).
